# knobs_9x9.env -- mission ktg-train, node arxiv-1902.10565::derive_cycle_knobs_9x9.
# The production cycle knobs, and the single source of truth for them.
# Obligations o24_cycle_knobs_derived and the knob conjunct of o13_loop_config_paths.
#
# Sourceable: `set -a; . knobs_9x9.env; set +a` exports every knob into the
# ${VAR:-default} block of codes/loop/synchronous_loop_9x9.sh (CHANGE 9), whose
# defaults are identical to the values here (asserted by
# `codes/eval/derive_knobs.py --assert-loop-defaults`).
#
# NOTHING HERE IS HAND-PICKED (DESIGN section 8 R14). Every value is the solution of a
# constraint read out of the reference code at path:line, evaluated at a number some job
# actually measured. The set was first derived from Slurm jobs 298712 / 299259 (the loop
# smoke) and NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE was RE-DERIVED on 2026-09-05 from 41 production cycles of
# job 301099; both sets of inputs are recorded below. Smoke numbers are read from the
# job-suffixed evidence copies
# evidence/smoke/{rows_per_game,throughput_smoke,nlwp_max,audit}-298712/299259.* -- never
# from the unsuffixed names, which attempt 2 overwrote (obligation o37).
# Re-check the CURRENT set against the PRODUCTION measurement with:
#   python3 codes/eval/check_knobs_9x9.py \
#     --throughput evidence/production_chain/throughput.json \
#     --rows-file  evidence/production_chain/rows_per_game.txt \
#     --marginal-nets 3 --trend-nets 9 --horizon-nets 10
# and against the frozen smoke evidence with the parameter-free
#   python3 codes/eval/check_knobs_9x9.py
# The underlying arithmetic, at either set of inputs:
#   python3 codes/eval/derive_knobs.py \
#     --rows-per-game 18.5249 --rows-per-game-lower 16.9968 \
#     --reuse 8 --samples-per-epoch 20000 --games 1500 --keep 120000 --cap 100000 \
#     --min-rows 25000 --taper 50000 --batch 128 --cpus 32 --game-threads 18
#
# --gate-games-measured is needed only for the two -298712 / -299259 files, which were
# written before audit_smoke.py emitted `gatekeeper_games_total` (156 is that job's
# measured gatekeeper sgf line count, audit-298712.json S4_gatekeeper_sgfs.lines, and
# check_knobs_9x9.py reads it from there rather than taking it as an argument). Obligation
# o41: derive_knobs.py has no fallback for any measured key -- a missing one is an exit
# naming the key, never a constant.
#
# MEASURED INPUTS -- jobs 298712 (attempt 1) and 299259 (attempt 2, ppid-filtered sampler,
# no foreign pids). Cited by name: evidence/smoke/audit-298712.json and audit-299259.json;
# sha256 of every file read is in evidence/derive_cycle_knobs/derivation.md section 1.
#   r      = 32.3    rows/game, REAL net   (646 rows / 20 games, 298712 leg-D1 probe)
#                    corroborated 31.95 (639/20) and 34.4 (2064/60) by 299259's two probes;
#                    all three re-run in derivation.txt block 4 and none moves a knob
#   r_lo   = 25.0775 = r * (1 - 1/sqrt(20)), the section 11 lower bound used for every
#            binding inequality, so a real r below the 20-game measurement still holds
#   r0     = 31.675  rows/game, random net (2534 rows / 80 games)
#   r0_lo  = 28.1336 = r0 * (1 - 1/sqrt(80))
#   353.8  bytes/row on disk (896536 B / 2534 rows) -- SUPERSEDED by the production
#          366.27 B/row (350347842 B / 956527 rows); the smoke figure was 3.5 % optimistic
#          and every storage line below is now at 366.27. As measured it was 10.94 KiB/game at r0 and
#          11.16 KiB/game at r -- both above the 10 KiB/game c10 second conjunct, which
#          299259 refuted; the guard arithmetic below uses B/row, never KiB/game
#   2031.7 real-net selfplay games/h (299259, 80 real-net games / 141.75 s); 298712's
#          20-game probe gave 2341.5, and check_knobs_9x9.py takes the SLOWER of the two
#   14.243 train samples/s all-in (298712; torch.compile included); 299259 gave 17.202
#          and the check again takes the slower
#   threads: 22 random-net selfplay, 25 any CUDA-context stage, 14 train, 4+8 shuffle --
#          298712 and 299259 agree, and 299259's sampler had no foreign pids at all
#
# NOT USED, deliberately: nothing here is derived from cheapSearchProb or from assumption
# a07_moves_per_game_80. 299259 measured full_frac = 0.342 +- 0.006 over three runs against
# the 1 - cheapSearchProb = 0.25 the derivation.md section 4 arithmetic predicts, so the
# "(1-0.75)*80 + 0.02*80 ~ 22 rows/game" chain is refuted. Every row count below is the
# measured rows/game, which is what makes this derivation independent of that failure.

# --- the eleven loop-block knobs -------------------------------------------------

# RE-DERIVED 2026-09-05 FROM PRODUCTION, 1000 -> 1500 (mission.json decisions[6], human,
# 2026-09-05; applies from chain link 2 on). The 1000 above rested on r = 32.3 rows/game,
# which was a 20-game probe against the smoke's barely-trained net. Forty production
# cycles refute it: rows/game falls as the net learns to stop filling the board, and the
# escalation at status_log.txt read 4 measured
#   aggregate r = 22.174 over 36040 real-net games   -> 1000*r = 22174 < 1.2*E = 24000
#   MARGINAL  r = 18.525 over the last three complete net dirs (8000 games)
# T1 (freshness) was violated at 1000 games: a cycle no longer produced even ONE epoch's
# worth of new rows (18525 < E = 20000). The mechanism is measured, not guessed:
# moves/game collapsed 130.8 -> ~81 between the first and third accepted net and has sat
# at 80-82 since (a one-time transition, over), while rows/move drifts 0.252 -> 0.227 on
# top of it. Resignations are 0.0 % in EVERY net directory, so the shortening is not
# resignation.
#
# G is the solution of the same two inequalities, now at the MARGINAL r -- the rate the
# NEXT cycle actually gets, the whole-run aggregate being flattered by the early
# long-game nets:
#   freshness (T1)   G >= 1.2*E / r     = 24000 / 18.525 = 1295.6
#                    G >= 1.2*E / r_lo  = 24000 / 16.997 = 1412.0   <- binding
#   bootstrap (K5)   G >= min_rows / r0_lo = 25000 / 31.028 = 805.7
# max = 1412.0, rounded up to 1500. At 1500: 1500*18.525 = 27787 rows/cycle, +15.78 % on
# 1.2*E; at r_lo 1500*16.997 = 25495, +6.23 %. The set survives any r >= 24000/1500 = 16.0.
#
# r_lo = 16.997 is NOT the sampling formula r*(1-1/sqrt(n)) this file used for the 20-game
# probe. Over the 8000 games the marginal rests on, that formula's term is 1.12 % -- it
# bounds SAMPLING error, and sampling error is no longer the binding uncertainty. DRIFT
# is: r falls as the net trains. So r_lo is the marginal carried TEN accepted nets forward
# at the measured least-squares slope of rows/game over the last nine complete net
# directories, -0.1528 rows/game per accepted net (per_net table in
# evidence/production_chain/throughput.json; reproduced by
# `check_knobs_9x9.py --marginal-nets 3 --trend-nets 9 --horizon-nets 10`). Ten accepted
# nets is ~31 cycles at the measured acceptance cadence (13 acceptances in 41 cycles),
# i.e. ~9 h of link 2 -- several times the demonstrated monitoring interval (reads 1-4
# spanned 7.7 h). The newest net directory is excluded from every fit: it is the net still
# playing and its npz files lag its own sgfs, which is why the same 18035 rows read as
# 15.88 rows/game in the read-4 table (1136 games) and 17.34 in rows_per_game.txt (1040).
#
# [OPEN] the drift has NOT stopped. At -0.1528 rows/game per accepted net the marginal
# reaches 16.0 -- where T1 fails again at 1500 games -- after ~16.5 further accepted nets,
# ~52 cycles. This knob must be re-derived at the monitoring read that sees the marginal
# cross ~17.0, and node scale_data_window owns the structural fix (raising min_rows /
# lowering E, rather than buying freshness with ever more games).
NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE=1500

# Upstream :58, unchanged. Measured 4-thread parent + 8 single-thread workers = 12 threads.
NUM_THREADS_FOR_SHUFFLING=8

# E/batch = 156 >= 100 = print_train_loss_every_batches (train.py:1379), so
# metrics_train.json is written at all -- at the smoke's 38 batches/epoch it stayed 0 bytes.
# The floor is 100*128 = 12800; 20000 is that rounded up to a round number, and is also
# DESIGN section 2's pilot value. Upstream :59 = 100000 (a 19x19 number).
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH=20000

# Upstream :60, the hard reuse cap; never raised (task file section 13). The set actually
# uses 3.10 (lower90 3.99), so a 2x error in r still cannot cross 8.
MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA=8

# = NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_EPOCH // 2, which is train.py:441's own default made explicit.
NUM_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_SWA=10000

# Upstream :62, unchanged. Peak VRAM was 4094 MiB at batch 32 on a 180 GB B200, so 128
# has ~40x headroom; and 156 batches/epoch is what the metrics floor above needs.
BATCHSIZE=128

# = 1.25 * E. In cycle 1 the shuffle window IS min_rows (shuffle.py:1077 caps the usable
# random rows there, and :414-435 then returns exactly min_rows), so min_rows alone decides
# whether cycle 1 can fill an epoch: train.py:1303-1346 returns None -- and -quit-if-no-data
# exits 0 with NO export (:1487-1488) -- below round(E/batch) batches. 25000 rows = 195
# batches against the 156 an epoch needs. Reachable: 1500 * r0_lo = 46542 >= 25000 at the
# production random-net r0_lo (31.028 over 5000 games; the smoke's 28.134 gave 42201).
# UNCHANGED by the 2026-09-05 re-derivation, and not even consulted by chain link 2: this
# knob binds only a cycle-1 RANDOM bootstrap, and link 2 resumes a mature window pinned at
# SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS = 120000 rows, five times min_rows. Upstream :63 = 100000.
SHUFFLE_MINROWS=25000

# = 5 * E, keeping upstream's own cap/epoch ratio of 5 (:64 500000 / :59 100000). This knob
# is what fixes EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT, because train.py:1257 caps the bucket at
# max(MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, E) and :1434 spends E per epoch. The ratio survives the
# measurement: 5*E = 100000 <= (MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA/2) * G * r_lo = 100310, i.e. even at half
# the reuse cap and at the conservative r the cycle's own new data pays for all five epochs.
MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE=100000

# Upstream :65, unchanged: no measured constraint binds it. It only sets where the shuffle
# window's growth turns sublinear (shuffle.py:421); at 9x9 scale the window is still in its
# near-linear regime (window ~ min_rows + 0.4 * post-random rows) for the first ~20 cycles.
TAPER_WINDOW_SCALE=50000

# = 1.2 * MAX_TRAIN_SAMPLES_PER_CYCLE, keeping upstream's keep/cap ratio (:66 600000 / :64
# 500000). Satisfies the shipped rule keep > cap (:66) and holds a whole cycle's draw
# (5*E = 100000 rows) with 20 % margin. Upstream :66 = 600000.
SHUFFLE_KEEPROWS=120000

# = floor(min(gain, cap_eff) / E) with gain = G*r*MAX_TRAIN_PER_DATA = 258400 (lower90
# 200620) and cap_eff = max(cap, E) = 100000 -> 5, the same at r and at r_lo.
# Passed to train.py as BOTH -epochs-per-export and -max-epochs-this-instance, so the
# persistent export_cycle_counter (train.py:871,975,1743,1831) can advance by at most 5 per
# cycle: never more than ONE candidate per cycle, from cycle 1 on.
# THE RAMP (obligation o40, corrected 2026-09-04): while models/ is empty every cycle plays
# the random net and shuffle.py:1077 caps its usable rows at SHUFFLE_MINROWS, so the window
# is pinned at 25000 rows = ONE epoch per cycle (-no-repeat-files ends the instance when the
# shuffled files run out, training_data_generator.py:35; -quit-if-no-data then exits 0 with
# no export, train.py:1487-1489). The persistent counter therefore needs five such cycles:
# the FIRST candidate exports at cycle 5 and is gated at cycle 6, real-net rows exist only
# from the cycle whose gate ACCEPTS, and exactly one export per cycle begins at cycle 16
# under acceptance at 6 (later per rejection). No knob moves: every K1-K7/T1-T4 inequality is
# evaluated at cycle 1's window, which a random-net cycle never goes below.
# Re-derive the table with codes/eval/derive_knobs.py (EXPORT RAMP BY CYCLE).
EPOCHS_PER_EXPORT=5

# --- the two allocation-side knobs, coupled to the ones above --------------------
# These are NOT read by synchronous_loop_9x9.sh. They are the numbers that
# codes/loop/loop.sbatch (#SBATCH --cpus-per-task, REQ_CPUS) and codes/cfg/{selfplay,
# gatekeeper}_9x9.cfg (numGameThreads) must carry; wiring them is o38, owned by
# loop_resume_under_walltime / cfg_9x9_override, not by this node.
#
# DESIGN section 1's [BLOCKING]: a 24-CPU declaration is false for any stage holding a CUDA
# context -- job 298712 measured nlwp_max 25 on both real-net stages against the 22 that
# every random-net selfplay measured, the +3 being CUDA runtime threads that
# debugSkipNeuralNet never creates (setup.cpp:126). Two repairs were admissible; with the
# CPU cap withdrawn (mission.json decisions[0]) raising the declaration is free, while
# cutting numGameThreads would lower the NN queue depth that DESIGN section 1 already calls
# the reason the GPU sits at 21.5 % mean duty. So the game threads stay and the declaration
# rises.
#
# Thread budget at numGameThreads 18, worst stage first:
#   gatekeeper, two real nets  18 + 2 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 main + 2x3 CUDA = 28
#   selfplay, real net + switch 18 + 1 nnServer + 1 dataWrite + 1 modelLoad + 1 main
#                               + 3 CUDA = 25 (measured), + 2 net-switch transient = 27
#   train                       14 (measured, OMP_NUM_THREADS=MKL_NUM_THREADS=4)
#   shuffle                     4-thread parent + 8 worker processes = 12 (measured)
# max = 28 <= 32, headroom 4. (The 28 is the sum of the terms on the gatekeeper line; the 29
# printed here before 2026-09-04 mis-added them -- obligation o40. Nothing downstream moves:
# the declaration was already 32 and T4 asserts worst <= 32.) One node has 124 CPUs and there
# is no CPU cap; the rule that survives is only "declared >= used".
KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK=32
KTG_NUM_GAME_THREADS=18

# --- projections that come with this set (not knobs; recorded so they can be refuted) ---
# MEASURED over 41 production cycles, job 301099 on l40s node gl111
# (evidence/production_chain/throughput.json), superseding the smoke projections:
#   real-net selfplay 5520.0 games/h  (36040 real-net games over the 37 cycle selfplay
#                     phases from cycle 6, the first gate, on; 23504.35 s). The file's
#                     all-net 6174.2 credits the 5 random-bootstrap cycles, which ran
#                     1000 games in ~85 s each, and is NOT the rate link 2 gets.
#   train             1964.646 samples/s   (3860608 samples / 1965.04 s)
#   gate              0.59638 s per gate game (938.11 s / 1573 gate games)
#   bytes/row         366.27 B on disk
# cycle wall  =  978 s selfplay (1500 games at 5520.0 games/h, NO derate: the 0.5x was an
#                     allowance for a 20-game probe, and this is 37 production cycles)
#             +   51 s train (5*20000 samples at 1964.6 samples/s)
#             +  239 s gate + 82 s shuffle + 60 s export = 1410 s = 0.392 h
#             -> 182 whole cycles inside one 2-23:30:00 chain link. [PRELIMINARY]
#                The DIRECTLY measured production cycle is 0.199 h at 1000 games; this
#                model is the more pessimistic of the two because its gate term charges
#                every cycle for a two-net gate at 200 games, where production gates every
#                ~2.8 cycles at ~121 games.
# storage     = 15.89 MiB/cycle monotonic (tdata 9.70 + sgfs 3.33 + one export 2.87)
#             + 557 MiB bounded steady state (3 shuffle dirs, 10 rejected models,
#               10 checkpoints, 3 dated archives)
#             -> 23.37 GiB after a full 182-cycle link against the 500 GiB cap
#                (KTG_SCRATCH_HARD_BYTES in codes/data_budget/budget.env); 30893 cycles
#                would fit before the cap.
#
# [OPEN] the storage model UNDER-counts: production actually grew runs/p1 to 1688046145 B
#        in 41 cycles = 41.2 MiB/cycle, 2.6x this model, and the mission root sits at
#        13.82 GB (2.6 % of the cap). T3 holds by three orders of magnitude either way;
#        the gap is a defect of the model, owned by data_budget / measure_stage_throughput.
# [OPEN] the rows/game drift has NOT stopped -- see the NUM_GAMES_PER_CYCLE block.
# [CLOSED 2026-09-05] real-net games/hour and train samples/s, which used to rest on 100
#        probe games and one probe train: 5520.0 games/h and 1964.646 samples/s over 41
#        production cycles.
# [CLOSED 2026-09-05] the two-real-net gatekeeper's thread count, which used to be
#        arithmetic (28) on a one-net measurement of 25: production measured nlwp_max 24
#        on the gatekeeper over 15 gate stages, 24 selfplay, 13 train, 4 shuffle, all
#        ppid-filtered, against the declared 32. KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK 32 has headroom 8.
# WIRED 2026-09-04 (o39): codes/loop/loop.sbatch declares --cpus-per-task=32, reads REQ_CPUS
#        from KTG_CPUS_PER_TASK in this file, and refuses to start a link whose granted
#        SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK or whose cfg numGameThreads disagrees with these two values.
